  "LEGAL STANDARD": ["heading", "framing"],
  "To survive a motion to dismiss under Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6), a complaint must plead “enough facts to state a claim to relief that is plausible on its face.” Bell Atl. Corp. v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544, 570, 127 S. Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929 (2007).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The Supreme Court has explained the standard this way:": ["other", "framing"],
  "A claim has facial plausibility when the plaintiff pleads factual content that allows the court to draw the reasonable inference that the defendant is liable for the misconduct alleged.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The plausibility standard is not akin to a “probability requirement,” but it asks for more than a sheer possibility that a defendant has acted unlawfully.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Ashcroft v. Iqbal, 556 U.S. 662, 678, 129 S. Ct. 1937, 173 L. Ed. 2d 868 (2009) (citation omitted).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The Court accepts well-pleaded factual allegations as true, but it does not credit “[t]hreadbare recitals of the elements of a cause of action, supported by mere conclusory statements.” Id.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "In Iqbal, the Court applied that standard to dismiss a complaint whose allegations of discriminatory purpose were conclusory.": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "The Court may also consider documents incorporated by reference in the complaint or integral to it, such as the Agreement. Chambers v. Time Warner, Inc., 282 F.3d 147, 152–53 (2d Cir. 2002).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "ARGUMENT": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Corvina’s fraud and Section 349 claims fail for independent reasons.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "Point I shows that the fraud claim is not pleaded with particularity and in any event duplicates the contract claim.": [
    "roadmap",
    "framing"
  ],
  "Point II shows that the Section 349 claim concerns a private contract, not conduct aimed at consumers.": [
    "roadmap",
