  ],
  "I. THE FRAUDULENT INDUCEMENT CLAIM (COUNT II) SHOULD BE DISMISSED": ["heading", "conclusion"],
  "Count II fails for two independent reasons.": ["conclusion", "conclusion"],
  "A. Corvina Does Not Plead Fraud with the Particularity Rule 9(b) Requires": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "A party alleging fraud “must state with particularity the circumstances constituting fraud.” Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(b).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "To satisfy the rule, a complaint must “(1) specify the statements that the plaintiff contends were fraudulent, (2) identify the speaker, (3) state where and when the statements were made, and (4) explain why the statements were fraudulent.” Lerner v. Fleet Bank, N.A., 459 F.3d 273, 290 (2d Cir. 2006) (internal quotation marks omitted).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Courts in this District routinely dismiss fraud claims that attribute a statement to an unnamed “representative” on an unspecified date. See Meridian Produce Co. v. Talbot Freight Sys., Inc., No. 18-cv-7702 (TZW), 2019 WL 1234567, at *3 (S.D.N.Y. Mar. 5, 2019) (dismissing fraud claim against carrier where shipper alleged only that “a salesperson” promised refrigerated service “in or around 2017”); see also Rombach v. Chang, 355 F.3d 164, 170 (2d Cir. 2004) (applying Rule 9(b) to any claim that “sounds in fraud”). But see Kessler v. Northgate Cold Storage, LLC, 2021 WL 4410382, at *6 (S.D.N.Y. Sept. 27, 2021) (sustaining fraud claim where the plaintiff identified the speaker by title and the statement by month).": [
    "explanation",
    "law"
  ],
  "Corvina’s allegations fail each of these requirements.": ["application", "application"],
  "The Complaint attributes the “fully temperature-controlled fleet” statement to an unnamed “sales representative” and places it only “in early 2025.” (Compl. ¶ 9.)": [
    "application",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Like the shipper in Meridian Produce, Corvina does not say who spoke, when, or where. Meridian Produce, 2019 WL 1234567, at *3.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "Unlike the plaintiff in Kessler, Corvina does not even give the speaker’s title.": [
    "counter",
    "application"
  ],
  "Its allegation that Halvorsen “knew” some trailers lacked monitoring units is a bare assertion of the kind Iqbal disregards. See Iqbal, 556 U.S. at 678; Twombly, 550 U.S. at 555.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "And it pleads no facts suggesting the statement was false when made: a single refrigeration failure months later says nothing about the state of Halvorsen’s fleet in early 2025. Cf. Arista Records, LLC v. Doe 3, 604 F.3d 110, 120 (2d Cir. 2010) (noting that pleading on information and belief is permitted only where the facts are peculiarly within the defendant’s possession).": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "The fraud claim should therefore be dismissed.": ["conclusion", "conclusion"],
  "B. The Fraud Claim Duplicates the Breach of Contract Claim": ["heading", "conclusion"],
  "Under New York law, a fraud claim that arises from the same facts as a contract claim must be dismissed unless the plaintiff shows a legal duty separate from the contract, a fraudulent misrepresentation collateral or extraneous to the contract, or special damages not recoverable in contract. Bridgestone/Firestone, Inc. v. Recovery Credit Servs., Inc., 98 F.3d 13, 20 (2d Cir. 1996).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Here, the alleged misrepresentation concerns the very performance the Agreement promises: refrigerated carriage between 32 and 38 degrees. Agreement § 4.2.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "The damages Corvina seeks, the value of the spoiled load, are the same damages it seeks for breach. (Compl. ¶¶ 41, 49.)": [
    "application",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Corvina may respond that the representative described a present fact rather than a future promise.": [
    "counter",
    "application"
  ],
  "That distinction does not help it, because the Agreement itself warrants the same capability, so the statement is not collateral to the contract. See Bridgestone/Firestone, 98 F.3d at 20.": [
    "counter",
    "application"
  ],
  "Corvina’s fraud claim is thus the contract claim under another name, and it should be dismissed for this reason as well.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "II. THE SECTION 349 CLAIM (COUNT III) FAILS BECAUSE THE CONDUCT ALLEGED IS NOT CONSUMER-ORIENTED": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "Section 349 prohibits “[d]eceptive acts or practices in the conduct of any business, trade or commerce.” N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 349(a).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "A plaintiff must show, as a threshold matter, that the challenged conduct was “consumer-oriented.” Oswego Laborers’ Local 214 Pension Fund v. Marine Midland Bank, N.A., 85 N.Y.2d 20, 25, 647 N.E.2d 741, 623 N.Y.S.2d 529 (1995).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Private contract disputes unique to the parties do not fall within the statute. Id.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Corvina alleges a negotiated, multi-year freight agreement between two businesses, with its own price schedule and liability cap. (Compl. ¶¶ 12–15.)": [
    "application",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "That is the paradigm of a private dispute, and Count III should be dismissed.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
